<!-- ===================== SECTION 6: MAURYAN EMPIRE ===================== -->
<h2 id="mauryan">6. Mauryan Empire (321-185 BCE)</h2>

<p>The <span class="highlight">Mauryan Empire</span> was the first pan-Indian empire, stretching from the Hindu Kush in the northwest to Bengal in the east, and from the Himalayas in the north to the Deccan plateau in the south. It was the largest empire in Indian history until the British Raj, covering approximately 5 million square kilometres. The Mauryan period marks the first time most of the Indian subcontinent was politically unified. The empire was founded by <strong>Chandragupta Maurya</strong> in 321 BCE with the guidance of his chief minister <strong>Chanakya</strong> (also called Kautilya or Vishnugupta), the author of the <strong>Arthashastra</strong> — one of the most sophisticated treatises on statecraft, economics, and military strategy ever written. The Arthashastra remains relevant for understanding ancient Indian governance, taxation, diplomacy (mandala theory), and espionage.</p>

<h3>Foundation and Sources</h3>

<p>Greek sources (<strong>Megasthenes' Indica</strong> — through fragments preserved by Strabo, Arrian, Pliny, Diodorus Siculus; Megasthenes was the Seleucid ambassador to Chandragupta's court) and Indian sources (<strong>Arthashastra</strong> — rediscovered by R. Shamasastry in 1905, 15 books, 180 chapters; <strong>Mudrarakshasa</strong> — a Sanskrit play by Vishakhadatta, c. 5th cent. CE, describing Chanakya's political machinations; <strong>Ashoka's edicts</strong> — 14 Major Rock Edicts, Minor Rock Edicts, Pillar Edicts, Cave Inscriptions, the first deciphered texts in Brahmi script, deciphered by James Prinsep in 1837; Puranic genealogies; Buddhist texts like the Mahavamsa and Dipavamsa of Sri Lanka) provide our knowledge. The empire collapsed 137 years after Chandragupta's accession.</p>

<h3>Chandragupta Maurya (321-297 BCE)</h3>

<p>Chandragupta Maurya, with Chanakya's guidance, overthrew the unpopular Nanda king Dhana Nanda and captured Pataliputra. He then defeated the Greek satrapies (Seleucus Nicator, Alexander's successor, c. 305 BCE — Seleucus ceded the territories of Arachosia (Kandahar), Gedrosia (Balochistan), Paropamisadae (Kabul Valley), and Aria (Herat) to Chandragupta, gave his daughter in marriage, and received 500 war elephants in return). Chandragupta's empire extended from Afghanistan to Bengal and from the Himalayas to the Narmada. He established a highly centralised bureaucracy and a standing army (600,000 infantry, 30,000 cavalry, 9,000 elephants — according to Pliny's Natural History). He is said to have abdicated and become a Jain monk under Bhadrabahu (the 8th-century CE text Rajavali Kathe describes this) at Shravanabelagola, Karnataka, where he died by sallekhana (voluntary fasting).</p>

<h3>Bindusara (297-272 BCE)</h3>

<p>Chandragupta's son, called <strong>Amitraghata</strong> ('Slayer of Enemies') by Greek authors. He expanded the empire southwards into the Deccan as far as Karnataka and Andhra (the 'Southern Mauryan provinces' with capital at Suvarnagiri). He patronised the Ajivika sect (a shramana movement founded by Makkhali Gosala). He received the Greek ambassador <strong>Deimachus</strong> from Seleucid king Antiochus I. Bindusara asked Antiochus to send him sweet wine, dried figs, and a Sophist philosopher — Antiochus sent the first two but politely refused the philosopher. Bindusara's reign was mostly peaceful — he maintained the administrative system his father had built and kept diplomatic relations with the Hellenistic world.</p>

<h3>Ashoka the Great (268-232 BCE - actual coronation 269 BCE)</h3>

<p><span class="highlight">Ashoka</span> (Devanampriya Priyadarshi — 'Beloved of the Gods, of Gracious Mien') is the most famous ruler in ancient Indian history. He was not the eldest son of Bindusara — he was appointed governor of Taxila and later Ujjain. After Bindusara's death, a succession war lasted 4 years before Ashoka prevailed (the Buddhist text Ashokavadana says he killed 99 of his brothers, but this is likely exaggerated — only two brothers, Susima and Vigatashoka, are named in reliable sources). His coronation was delayed until 269 BCE. In the 8th year of his reign (c. 261 BCE), he conquered <strong>Kalinga</strong> (modern Odisha) — a rich, independent maritime kingdom that had resisted Magadha since Ajatashatru's time. The Kalinga War was the turning point of Ashoka's life.</p>

<p><strong>The Kalinga War (c. 261 BCE)</strong>: The 13th Major Rock Edict describes the horror: '150,000 people were deported, 100,000 were killed, and many more died.' The Edict records Ashoka's deep remorse: 'Even the one-hundredth part or the one-thousandth part of all the people who suffered... would be considered deplorable now.' Ashoka renounced war forever, embraced <span class="highlight">Buddhism</span> (he became an upasaka — lay devotee — but maintained the Sangha, and later became a full bhikkhu according to some Sri Lankan sources), and adopted the policy of <strong>Dhamma</strong> (Dharma — righteous conduct, moral law, not Buddhist doctrine per se but a universal ethical code). He became the only military ruler in world history to abandon conquest after a military victory.</p>

<h4>Ashoka's Dhamma</h4>

<p>Ashoka's policy of <span class="highlight">Dhamma</span> was not a state religion but a universal code of social ethics carved into stone pillars and rock faces across the empire for all to read (14 Major Rock Edicts, Minor Rock Edicts, 7 Pillar Edicts, 4 Minor Pillar Edicts, 2 Tarai Pillar Edicts, 3 Cave Dedications). Key principles: <strong>Ahimsa</strong> (non-violence) — no animal sacrifices, reduced meat in royal kitchens, forbade killing of certain animals (parrots, geese, bats, tortoises, porcupines, squirrels, etc., listed in the 5th Pillar Edict — 25 categories of animals were protected). <strong>Tolerance</strong> — 'All sects deserve reverence for one reason or another' (12th Major Rock Edict). <strong>Social responsibility</strong> — appointed <strong>Dhamma-mahamattas</strong> (officers of righteousness) to enforce Dhamma, promote welfare, and supervise charitable institutions. <strong>Non-aggression</strong> — renounced war, established friendly relations with all kingdoms (including the five Hellenistic kings: Antiochus II Theos of Syria, Ptolemy II Philadelphos of Egypt, Antigonus Gonatas of Macedonia, Magas of Cyrene, Alexander of Epirus). <strong>Public works</strong> — dug wells, planted shade trees along roads, established hospitals (for both humans and animals — among the first animal hospitals in world history), built rest houses.</p>

