<h3>Mauryan Administration</h3>

<p>The Arthashastra provides the blueprint for Mauryan governance. The empire was divided into several provinces: the metropolitan province (with Pataliputra as capital), and four provincial capitals — <strong>Taxila</strong> (northwest), <strong>Ujjain</strong> (west), <strong>Suvarnagiri</strong> (south — the gold mines), <strong>Tosali</strong> (east/Kalinga). Provinces were ruled by <strong>kumaras</strong> (royal princes) who held the title of viceroy. The king was the head of state, aided by a council of ministers (mantri-parishad) including the chief minister (mukhya-mantrin), commander-in-chief (senapati), chief priest (purohita), chief judge (pradvivaka), treasurer (sannidhata), collector-general (samaharta), and the chief superintendent of the royal harem (antahpura-adhyaksha).</p>

<p><strong>Central Administration</strong>: The <strong>Arthashastra</strong> describes 28 superintendents (adhyakshas) in charge of various departments: agriculture (sita-adhyaksha), mines (akara-adhyaksha), forests (kupyadhyaksha), cattle (govyadhyaksha), trade (panya-adhyaksha), tolls (shulka-adhyaksha), weights and measures (pautava-adhyaksha), spinning and weaving (sutra-adhyaksha), liquor (suradhyaksha), shipping (navadhyaksha), precious metals (lakshanadhyaksha), passports (mudradhyaksha), and the superintendent of the city (nagaraka). The <strong>gopa</strong> was the village accountant responsible for 5-10 villages; the <strong>sthanika</strong> was the district officer. Each village had a <strong>gramika</strong> (village headman). Tax collectors: <strong>samaharta</strong> (collector-general), <strong>pradesikas</strong> (provincial revenue officers), <strong>rajukas</strong> (survey and assessment officers). Land revenue was 1/4th to 1/6th of produce (bhaga). Urban administration in Pataliputra was managed by 6 boards of 5 members each (Megasthenes' Indica): industries, trade and commerce, public records, inspections and licensing, public welfare (foreigners, births, deaths), and religious affairs.</p>

<h3>Mauryan Economy</h3>

<p>Agriculture was the backbone — the state owned vast crown lands (sita) farmed by labourers and slaves. <strong>Irrigation</strong> was state-managed (the Sudarshana lake in Junagadh, Gujarat, was built by the Mauryan governor of Saurashtra, Pushyagupta, using local labour; later repaired by Junagadh's Shaka ruler Rudradaman I in 150 CE — recorded in the Junagadh Rock Inscription of Rudradaman). <strong>Trade</strong> flourished with the Hellenistic world, Southeast Asia (Suvarnabhumi — the Golden Land), and Sri Lanka (Tambapanni). Major exports: spices, pearls, gemstones, cotton textiles, ivory, sandalwood, tortoise shell, peacocks, monkeys, and elephants (the elephant trade was a state monopoly). Major imports: horses (from Central Asia and Arabia), gold, silver, glassware, wine, and fine cloth. The state regulated trade through the superintendent of commerce (panyadhyaksha) who set prices, fixed profit margins (5% for local goods, 10% for foreign goods), and prevented hoarding. <strong>Coinage</strong>: punch-marked silver coins (pana), copper and silver karshapana — these are the earliest documented coins in India with symbols (sun, peacock, mountain, elephant, tree-in-railing, etc.). The state mint (lakshanadhyaksha) bore the royal mark. <strong>Industries</strong>: textiles (silk from Varanasi, cotton from Bengal), metallurgy (iron for tool-making, gold and silver for coinage and ornaments), ivory carving, gem-cutting, pottery (high-quality Northern Black Polished Ware — NBPW).</p>

<h3>Mauryan Art and Architecture</h3>

<p>The Mauryan period saw the first monumental stone architecture in India. <strong>Ashoka's pillars</strong>: monolithic sandstone pillars (polished to a mirror-like finish — the 'Mauryan polish') quarried at Chunar and transported hundreds of kilometres. Each pillar weighed 40-50 tons and stood 12-15 metres high. The capital has four lions back-to-back (the national emblem of India — adopted from the Sarnath pillar in 1950) or other animals (bull at Rampurva, elephant at Sankissa). The <strong>Sarnath Lion Capital</strong> is the finest example — 4 lions on a circular abacus with 4 animals (elephant, horse, bull, lion) separated by dharma-chakras. <strong>Stupas</strong>: Ashoka built 84,000 stupas (according to Sri Lankan tradition) containing Buddha's relics. The original brick stupa at Sanchi was built by Ashoka; it was later expanded by the Sungas and Satavahanas. <strong>Palace of Pataliputra</strong>: Megasthenes describes the wooden palace as more magnificent than the Persian palaces of Susa and Ecbatana — a hypostyle hall with 80 sandstone pillars. <strong>Cave architecture</strong>: Ashoka gave three caves to the Ajivika sect (Lomas Rishi Cave, Sudama Cave, Karna Chaupar Cave at Barabar Hills, Bihar — the earliest rock-cut caves in India, with polished interiors and a distinctive 'chaitya arch' facade). <strong>Column of Ashoka at Allahabad</strong>: now standing within the Allahabad Fort, it also bears inscriptions of Samudragupta and Jahangir — thus, containing records of three different dynasties (Maurya, Gupta, Mughal).</p>

<h3>Decline of the Mauryan Empire</h3>

<ul>
<li><strong>Weak successors after Ashoka</strong>: His sons and grandsons were weak rulers — Kunala (who was blinded by a rival queen according to the Ashokavadana), Samprati (the grandson, who became a Jain), Dasharatha (maintained Ajivika patronage), Samprati's successors (Salisuka, Devavarman, Shatadhanvan, Brihadratha) were incapable of ruling the vast empire.</li>
<li><strong>Division of the empire</strong>: After Ashoka's death, the empire was divided into western and eastern halves under his sons Tivala and Kunala (or Samprati and Dasharatha). The provinces (especially Taxila) began to break away.</li>
<li><strong>Financial crisis</strong>: The enormous expenditure on the army (the world's largest standing army at the time), a massive bureaucracy, and public works (hospitals, roads, wells) exhausted the treasury. The policy of dhamma reduced tax enforcement efficiency.</li>
<li><strong>Brahmanical reaction</strong>: Ashoka's pro-Buddhist policies (patronage to the Sangha, Buddhist missions) alienated the Brahmins who formed the traditional elite. The Arthashastra was abandoned in favour of the Dharmashastras. Pushyamitra Shunga, the last Mauryan commander-in-chief, assassinated the last Mauryan king Brihadratha (c. 185 BCE) at a military parade, and established the Shunga dynasty with a return to Vedic orthodoxy and Brahmanical sacrifice (including the Ashvamedha).</li>
<li><strong>Invasions</strong>: Greek (Indo-Greek/Bactrian Greeks under Demetrius and Menander moving into NW India c. 190 BCE), Parthian, and Scythian (Shaka) incursions into the northwest frontier were not effectively repelled by the post-Ashokan Mauryas.</li>
<li><strong>Over-centralisation</strong>: The empire was too large to be administered effectively from Pataliputra without modern communications. Provincial governors became autonomous. The system required a strong king at the centre, which the later Mauryas could not provide.</li>
</ul>

<div class="warn-box"><div class="warn-title">Historiographical Debate: Ashoka's Dhamma</div><div class="warn-text">Scholars disagree on whether Ashoka's Dhamma was a form of state Buddhism or a universal moral code unrelated to any religion. Romila Thapar argues that Dhamma was a policy of social integration aimed at unifying a diverse empire — it was political in intent, not religious. Other scholars (like G. C. Pande) argue that Dhamma was deeply influenced by Buddhism but was deliberately non-sectarian. The fact that Ashoka did not make Buddhism the state religion or persecute non-Buddhists (unlike Constantine's adoption of Christianity) supports Thapar's interpretation. However, his Bairat Edict recommends specific Buddhist texts, and his approach to the Sangha was paternalistic — he tried to weed out corrupt monks and bureaucratise the Sangha.</div></div>

